.TH TEST.CFG 5 "Version 4.3.30:  4 Sep 2019" "Xymon"
.SH NAME
test.cfg \- per-test settings and test groups
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B $XYMONHOME/etc/test.cfg

.SH DESCRIPTION
test.cfg holds settings that belong to one test (one status column),
in one place instead of spread over environment variables in
xymonserver.cfg(5), and declares test groups. The file is optional:
without it, or for a test it does not mention, everything works from
the environment as before.

.SH FILE FORMAT
The file is a list of blocks. A block is a keyword and a name, then
its contents between "{" and "}". Inside a block, each statement is a
keyword followed by its arguments, ended by a newline or ";". Blocks
nest. Whitespace is insignificant, "#" starts a comment, and a word
containing blanks can be quoted with double quotes.

.nf
    TEST myapp {
            HANDLER ncv
            GRAPHS myapp, myapp_errors
            METRIC myapp {
                    NCV requests:GAUGE errors:DERIVE
            }
    }

    group web {
            member http
            member https
            member apache
    }
.fi

A syntax error is reported in the log of the program reading the file,
with its line number, and the whole file is then ignored: every test
falls back to the environment.

.SH TEST BLOCKS
.BR "TEST name { ... }"
holds the settings for the test "name".

.IP "\fBHANDLER\fR handler"
The RRD handler that stores this test's data (see
.I xymond_rrd(8)),
for example "ncv" or "devmon". This takes precedence over the
TEST2RRD environment variable for this test.

.IP "\fBGRAPHS\fR graph[, graph ...]"
The graphs shown on this test's status page, replacing the
GRAPHS_<test> environment variable. Commas between the names are
optional.

.IP "\fBMETRIC\fR name { ... }"
A metric the test produces. When a test has exactly one METRIC and no
HANDLER, the metric binds the test to an RRD handler: "ncv" when the
metric carries an NCV or SPLITNCV statement, otherwise the handler
named like the metric. Such an implicit binding never replaces a
TEST2RRD mapping the environment already has for the test - the
environment's mapping is kept and a warning is logged; use HANDLER to
override it.
.PP
Inside a METRIC block:

.IP "\fBNCV\fR name:type [name:type ...]"
.IP "\fBSPLITNCV\fR name:type [name:type ...]"
The name-colon-value datasets to store, replacing the NCV_<test> or
SPLITNCV_<test> environment variable (see the NCV section of
.I xymond_rrd(8)).
The pairs may also be written as a block, one per statement:
"NCV { requests:GAUGE; errors:DERIVE }". An NCV statement without any
pair is ignored, with a warning.

.IP "\fBCOUNTLINES\fR"
Page this test's graphs by the number of lines in its status message,
as the tests listed in the \-\-multigraphs option of
.I svcstatus.cgi(1)
are (disk, inode and a few others by default).
.PP
TEST and METRIC blocks also accept SOURCE, CMD, INTERVAL, PORT,
TRACKMAX, and storage statements (EXCLUDE, STOREPATTERN, and blocks
naming a storage backend). This version reads but does not use them;
the graph-side storage filters are set in
.I graphs.cfg(5)
instead.

.SH TEST GROUPS
.BR "group name { member test ... }"
declares a group of tests shown together. A member statement may name
one test or several. The group name must not be the name of a test:
the group takes over that column on the overview pages and its
svcstatus.cgi page, so the test itself can no longer be viewed.

A group is for display only. It has no status of its own, sends no
alert and cannot be acknowledged: its members keep their own status,
history, alerts and acknowledgements.

On the overview pages generated by
.I xymongen(1),
a host's members are replaced by one column named after the group.
Its light shows the worst colour among the host's members, and links
to the group page: svcstatus.cgi with SERVICE=<group> shows a band
with each member's colour, followed by each member's status, and the
page itself takes the worst colour. A host with none of a group's
members shows no column for it.

When svcstatus.cgi runs with --access, a user allowed to view the
group sees only the members they are allowed to view: the others are
left out of the band, the sections and the page colour.

"rollup worst" may be written in a group; it is the only rollup, and
the default.

.SH WHEN CHANGES TAKE EFFECT
Each program reads the file once, when it first needs it. The CGIs and
xymongen start for every request or run, so they see a change at once;
xymond_rrd must be restarted to see it.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymonserver.cfg(5), xymond_rrd(8), graphs.cfg(5), xymongen(1),
svcstatus.cgi(1), xymon(7)
